  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1212 PM MST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nWe will be entering a prolonged relatively quiet period after today \nand likely through the end of next week as upper level ridging will \nreside over the western United States. For us, that will mean \noverall dry conditions and above normal temperatures. \n\nToday, light snow showers are possible (10-20% chance) mainly for \nthe I-70 mountain corridor and surrounding areas above \n8,500-9,000 ft this afternoon and evening. However, with overall \nlack of decent moisture, snow accumulations will be a dusting to \n<1\" at best. Across the plains, one last day of seasonal \ntemperatures are expected today, with forecasted highs in the \nmid-to-high 50s (and likely low 60s for the far eastern plains). \n\nThis weekend and into beginning of next week, upper level ridging \nwill continue entering the region with a cut-off low spinning over \nsouthern Arizona/New Mexico. With weak northerly flow aloft over \nColorado, we won't have access to the moisture from that system and \ninstead will receive drier air. This will keep precipitation chances \nnear-zero for our whole forecast area. In terms of temperatures, it \nsure won't really feel like fall, with temperatures expected to \nreach 10-13 degrees above normal. 700-mb temps will reach up to 7-8 \ndegrees C by Sunday and Monday, which roughly translates to \nsurface temperatures in the high 60s to potentially low 70s by \nMonday. \n\nEnsemble guidance is in excellent agreement (unfortunately) of above \nnormal temperatures and overall dry conditions lasting through the \nend of next week due to the upper level ridging in place. We could \nsee slightly \"cooler\" conditions (temperatures in the low 60s) on \nWednesday as northerly flow aloft advects colder air into the \nregion. However, we should warm right back up on Thursday. Towards \nnext weekend, the upper level ridge could begin to break down, but \nmodel solutions vary on exactly when that will happen.",
